WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:07.120
Well, let's take a look at a shawl collar
for a pullover. In these two examples,

2
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.480
the collar is really high and tight
and they look really uncomfortable.

3
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.680
Let's take a look at how we
can improve these collars.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.920
Here's a typical shawl collar neck

5
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.520
opening. It's basically a V neck
with a straight bottom. In this example,

6
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.480
the bottom is four inches and the neck
opening is a typical eight inch.

7
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:36.000
Which would fit my mannequin. If I lay
a four-inch wide collar along this edge,

8
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.760
we can see that it comes up really
high up against the neckline.

9
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.240
If we fold the collar in half,
it's still pretty high.

10
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.440
That collar is filling in that eight inch

11
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.360
neck opening and making it more
like a four inch neck opening.

12
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.680
And I don't want a turtleneck.

13
00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.400
By opening up the neck in this example,
four or five inches,

14
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.880
the angle changes and the folded
over collar will fit much better.

15
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.800
The lesson here is you can't use
a standard neck opening for a shawl

16
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.720
collar. You need to make it
wider so that collar will fit.

17
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.560
Here's another shawl collar variation,

18
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:28.120
the yellow collar is knit in 2x2,
ribbing in a very long, skinny strip.

19
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.560
It meets in the center front

20
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:38.120
when it's folded over the neck, it isn't
taking up as much space in the neckline.

21
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.440
That skinny band can also be overlapped
slightly in the front,

22
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.520
but you need to accommodate
that overlap in the neck opening.

23
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.000
Here's a wider collar knit in the other

24
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.200
direction, also in 2x2 ribbing,
it's been blocked heavily.

25
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.440
As in the previous drawings,
you can see that this collar fills

26
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:07.360
in that neck opening and comes up really
high at the neckline. ,ven folded over.

27
00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.680
It's pretty high and tight.

28
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.200
Making the neck opening wider will give

29
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.960
the collar more room and it'll
be much more comfortable.

30
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:26.760
As machine knitters, we need to consider
our needlebed limitations.

31
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.960
This piece is 200 stitches wide
and it won't go around the neck opening.

32
00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:39.800
I'd need to knit and seam to pieces
or knit this collar by hand.

33
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.160
There are many,
many variations of the shawl collar.

34
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.120
Look at this one closely and you can
see that it's knit in pieces.

35
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.560
The fronts are ribbed in one direction

36
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.240
and the back of the collar is
picked up, knit the other way.

37
00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:00.200
And then it's seamed. This is a great
solution for our needle bed limitations.

38
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.600
Also, notice the front edges of this

39
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.320
example are shaped,
forming a nice deep collar up

40
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:12.440
at the neckline while maintaining
a fairly narrow flat V opening.

